{
  "result": "On Tuesday the city installed a bench facing a brick wall. Everyone agreed it was a mistake. The river was behind it, along with the sycamores and the handsome old bridge. The wall belonged to a laundromat. Its only distinguishing feature was a pale rectangle where a sign had hung.\n\nBy Friday someone had drawn a small door inside the rectangle. It had a brass knob, a mail slot, and a welcome mat rendered in painstaking chalk. On Saturday a child sat on the bench and waited for somebody to come out. Her father sat beside her, holding two sandwiches.\n\nNobody came out. But the following week, an envelope appeared, tucked into a crack between the bricks. It was addressed to Whoever Lives Here. Inside was a recipe for lemon cake, with a note advising the reader to double the zest and not to apologize if the middle sank slightly.\n\nAfter that came postcards, a photograph of a dog asleep in a wheelbarrow, and a question about whether grief counted as a reason to cancel dinner. Someone wrote Yes beneath it in chalk. Someone else added, But tell us where to leave the soup. An address appeared the next morning.\n\nIn November a city worker arrived with a wrench to turn the bench around. He set his toolbox down and read the wall. Then he sat for a while. When he left, the bench was where it had always been, and a new note said, in block capitals: NICE VIEW.",
